## The core idea
Keyboard accessibility is the foundation everything else stands on. Screen reader users, switch users, voice control users and many people with motor disabilities all drive the page through keyboard events, directly or emulated. WCAG's requirements are short:

- **2.1.1 Keyboard (A)**: all functionality is operable with a keyboard.
- **2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap (A)**: focus can always move away with standard keys.
- **2.4.3 Focus Order (A)**: focus moves in an order that preserves meaning.
- **2.4.7 Focus Visible (AA)**: you can see where focus is.
- **2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (AA, new in 2.2)**: the focused element is not entirely hidden behind sticky headers, banners or chat widgets.
- **2.4.1 Bypass Blocks (A)**: a way to skip repeated content such as the navigation.

The engineering model: **there is exactly one focused element at a time. Tab and Shift+Tab move between interactive widgets; arrow keys move within a composite widget; Enter and Space activate; Escape dismisses.** Your job is to keep focus somewhere sensible at every moment, especially when the DOM changes under it.

## How tab order works
```text
Sequential focus navigation order
---------------------------------
1. Elements with tabindex > 0, in ascending tabindex value   <- avoid
2. Then everything focusable in DOM order:
   - natively focusable: a[href], button, input, select, textarea,
     summary, [contenteditable], iframe, audio/video with controls
   - anything with tabindex="0"
Not in sequence:
   - tabindex="-1": focusable by script (el.focus()) and click, skipped by Tab
   - disabled form controls, elements in inert subtrees, display:none,
     visibility:hidden
```
Rules that follow from this:
- **Tab order follows DOM order, not visual order.** CSS `order`, `flex-direction: row-reverse`, grid placement and absolute positioning can make the visual order disagree with the DOM. That breaks 2.4.3 and confuses sighted keyboard users. Fix the DOM order rather than patching with tabindex.
- **Never use positive tabindex.** It pulls elements to the front of the whole page order and is impossible to maintain.
- **`tabindex="0"`** only for custom interactive widgets that genuinely need focus. Making static text focusable "for screen readers" is wrong; screen readers read non-focusable content with their own virtual cursor.
- **`tabindex="-1"`** is for programmatic focus targets: a heading you move focus to after navigation, a dialog container, an error summary.

## Visible focus done properly
Browsers draw a default focus ring, and `:focus-visible` lets you show it only when the user is navigating by keyboard (or when the browser's heuristics decide it helps, for example text inputs always match).

```css
/* Remove the default only when you replace it */
:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid var(--ring);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

/* Never do this without a replacement */
/* *:focus { outline: none; } */

/* Keep focused elements clear of a sticky header (2.4.11) */
html {
  scroll-padding-top: 5rem; /* height of the sticky header plus margin */
}
```
Use `outline` rather than `box-shadow` for the main indicator: in Windows forced-colours (high contrast) mode, box shadows are removed but outlines are kept and recoloured. A transparent outline (`outline: 2px solid transparent`) is a known trick for components whose visible focus is drawn another way, so forced-colours users still get a ring. WCAG 2.4.13 (AAA) gives a measurable target: an indicator at least as large as a 2 CSS pixel perimeter with 3:1 contrast between focused and unfocused states.

## Skip links
A skip link is the first focusable element on the page and jumps past the header and navigation to the main content.

```tsx
// First thing inside <body>
<a href="#main" className="skip-link">Skip to main content</a>
<Header />
<main id="main" tabIndex={-1}>...</main>
```
```css
.skip-link {
  position: absolute;
  left: 1rem;
  top: -3rem;
}
.skip-link:focus {
  top: 1rem; /* visible when focused */
}
```
`tabIndex={-1}` on `main` ensures focus actually moves there in all browsers, so the next Tab continues from the main content rather than from the top. Landmarks and headings also satisfy 2.4.1 for screen reader users, but sighted keyboard users have no landmark shortcuts; the skip link is for them.

## Roving tabindex versus aria-activedescendant
Composite widgets (toolbars, tab lists, radio groups, menus, listboxes, grids, tree views) should be **one Tab stop**. Inside, arrow keys move. Otherwise a toolbar with 20 buttons costs 20 Tab presses to cross. There are two techniques.

```text
Roving tabindex                          aria-activedescendant
----------------------------------       ----------------------------------
Exactly one item has tabindex=0,         DOM focus stays on the container
the rest tabindex=-1.                    (or the input of a combobox).
Arrow key: move tabindex=0 to the        Arrow key: set aria-activedescendant
next item and call .focus() on it.       on the focused element to the id of
                                         the "virtually focused" option.
Real DOM focus moves: scrolling,         No DOM focus move: you must scroll
:focus styles, screen readers all        the item into view and style it
work naturally.                          yourself.
Best for: toolbars, tabs, radio          Best for: combobox listboxes, where
groups, menus, grids.                    typing must stay in the input.
```

A minimal roving-tabindex toolbar:
```tsx
function Toolbar({ items }: { items: { id: string; label: string; onSelect: () => void }[] }) {
  const [active, setActive] = useState(0);
  const refs = useRef<(HTMLButtonElement | null)[]>([]);

  const move = (next: number) => {
    const i = (next + items.length) % items.length;
    setActive(i);
    refs.current[i]?.focus();
  };

  return (
    <div
      role="toolbar"
      aria-label="Text formatting"
      onKeyDown={(e) => {
        if (e.key === "ArrowRight") move(active + 1);
        else if (e.key === "ArrowLeft") move(active - 1);
        else if (e.key === "Home") move(0);
        else if (e.key === "End") move(items.length - 1);
        else return;
        e.preventDefault();
      }}
    >
      {items.map((item, i) => (
        <button
          key={item.id}
          ref={(el) => { refs.current[i] = el; }}
          type="button"
          tabIndex={i === active ? 0 : -1}
          onClick={item.onSelect}
          onFocus={() => setActive(i)}
        >
          {item.label}
        </button>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```
The `onFocus` handler keeps state right when the user clicks an item. Tabbing away and back returns to the last active item, which is the expected behaviour in the ARIA Authoring Practices Guide's keyboard interface guidance.

## Focus traps (and when they are correct)
A **keyboard trap** that the user cannot escape is a WCAG failure. A **focus trap** inside a modal dialog is required behaviour: while a modal is open, Tab must cycle within it, Escape closes it, and when it closes focus returns to the element that opened it.

The modern platform does most of this for you:
```tsx
function ConfirmDelete({ onConfirm }: { onConfirm: () => void }) {
  const ref = useRef<HTMLDialogElement>(null);
  return (
    <>
      <button type="button" onClick={() => ref.current?.showModal()}>Delete project</button>
      <dialog ref={ref} aria-labelledby="del-title">
        <h2 id="del-title">Delete this project?</h2>
        <p>This cannot be undone.</p>
        <form method="dialog">
          <button value="cancel" autoFocus>Cancel</button>
          <button value="confirm" onClick={onConfirm}>Delete</button>
        </form>
      </dialog>
    </>
  );
}
```
`showModal()` puts the dialog in the top layer, makes the rest of the document **inert** (not focusable, not clickable, hidden from assistive tech), moves focus into the dialog, and closes on Escape. The HTML spec also restores focus to the previously focused element when the dialog closes. Before `<dialog>` was reliable, libraries implemented this with `aria-modal`, `aria-hidden` on siblings and a Tab-key loop. The `inert` attribute (supported in all major browsers since 2023) gives you the same "everything else is unreachable" effect for custom overlays: set `inert` on the app root while your overlay, rendered outside it, is open.

Non-modal things (a popover, a dropdown menu, a disclosure) should **not** trap focus. Tab moving out of a menu usually closes it. The `popover` attribute gives light-dismiss and top-layer rendering without trapping.

## Focus management in single-page apps
In a classic multi-page site, every navigation loads a new document: focus resets to the top and screen readers announce the new title. In an SPA, a client-side route change swaps content while focus stays on the link you clicked, which may no longer exist. A screen reader user hears nothing, and the next Tab may land somewhere random or back at the top.

Marcy Sutton's 2019 user testing of client-side routing with disabled users (on the Gatsby blog) compared several techniques and is worth reading in full. The common options in practice:
1. **Move focus to the new page's `h1`** (with `tabIndex={-1}`) or to a wrapper around the main content after each route change. The heading text is announced, and the next Tab continues from the new content.
2. **Announce the change** via a polite live region ("Navigated to Invoices") and leave focus on a stable element such as the skip link. Next.js ships a built-in route announcer that reads the new document title (falling back to the first `h1` or the path) on client navigations.
3. **Update `document.title`** on every route; it is what both approaches lean on, and it is required for 2.4.2.

```tsx
// Focus the page heading after client-side navigation (React Router style)
function PageHeading({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const ref = useRef<HTMLHeadingElement>(null);
  const { pathname } = useLocation();
  useEffect(() => {
    ref.current?.focus();
  }, [pathname]);
  return <h1 ref={ref} tabIndex={-1} className="focus:outline-none">{children}</h1>;
}
```
Do not move focus on the very first page load (the browser handles it) and do not hijack focus for in-page state changes like filtering a list; announce those instead (lesson 5).

**Other focus-loss traps in SPAs:**
- Deleting the focused row: move focus to the next row, or the previous, or the list heading, never leave it on `body`.
- Closing a custom popover: return focus to its trigger.
- Replacing a form with a success message: move focus to the message (`tabIndex={-1}`) or announce it.
- Virtualised lists unmounting the focused item while scrolling.

A quick check: run `document.activeElement` in the console after the action. If it is `body`, focus was lost.

## Trade-offs and when not to
- Arrow-key composites are only expected where APG defines them. A site navigation bar is a list of links; making it an arrow-key `menubar` forces an application interaction model on users who expect Tab.
- Moving focus is powerful and disruptive. Move it only on navigation, opening and closing overlays, and recovering from removed content.
- Single-key shortcuts (like "s" to search) conflict with screen reader and voice commands; 2.1.4 Character Key Shortcuts (A) requires a way to turn them off or remap them, or that they only work when the component has focus.

## Common mistakes and debugging
- Click handlers on non-focusable elements (`div onClick`): unreachable by keyboard.
- `onMouseDown`-only or hover-only interactions (menus that open on hover with no keyboard equivalent).
- Focus styles removed globally by a CSS reset.
- Modals rendered without inerting the background, so Tab wanders behind the overlay.
- Sticky headers covering the focused field when tabbing up the page (fix with `scroll-padding`).
- Hidden off-canvas menus that stay focusable when closed (`transform: translateX(-100%)` without `inert` or `visibility: hidden`).

Debugging: unplug the mouse. Tab through the entire flow, watching where focus goes. In Chrome DevTools you can show focused-element tracking, and logging `focusin` events on `document` reveals where focus is jumping.

## In the interview
**What they probe:** practical keyboard knowledge (tab order, tabindex values), whether you can build a modal correctly, and whether you understand SPA focus management. Seniors get "how would you make focus management consistent across a large app?"

**Model 60-second answer ("How do you handle focus when a modal opens and closes?"):**
1. On open, move focus inside: the first meaningful control or the dialog heading; prefer `<dialog>` with `showModal()`.
2. While open, the background is inert and Tab cycles within; Escape closes.
3. On close, return focus to the trigger (or a sensible fallback if it was removed).
4. Name the dialog with `aria-labelledby` pointing at its heading.
5. For custom overlays: render outside the app root, set `inert` on the root, restore focus manually.

**Follow-ups:** Roving tabindex versus aria-activedescendant? What is wrong with `tabindex="5"`? How do you handle route changes in a SPA? How do you keep focus visible but not show rings on mouse click?

## The core idea
Accessibility testing is a pyramid, just like functional testing, and the mistake most teams make is stopping at the bottom layer.

```text
                    /\
                   /  \   Usability testing with disabled users
                  /----\  (periodic, highest signal)
                 /      \  Manual screen reader passes
                /--------\ (NVDA + Firefox/Chrome, VoiceOver + Safari, mobile)
               /          \ Manual keyboard, zoom and reflow checks
              /------------\ (every feature, minutes each)
             /              \ Component tests with role-based queries
            /----------------\ + axe in unit, E2E and Storybook tests
           /                  \ Linting (eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y), typed
          /--------------------\ component APIs that require labels
```
Automated checks are cheap and catch regressions. They **cannot** judge meaning (is this alt text right?), flow (does focus go somewhere sensible after deleting a row?), or usability (can a screen reader user actually complete checkout?). Deque, the company behind axe-core, says its rules find about 57% of issues by volume in its audit data; the UK Government Digital Service's 2017 experiment, which planted known barriers in a test page, found every tool missed a large share of them. Either way: automation is necessary, not sufficient.

## Layer 1: static analysis
`eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y` catches problems in JSX before it runs: images without `alt`, `onClick` on non-interactive elements without keyboard handlers, invalid ARIA attributes, `aria-*` props that do not exist, anchors without `href`, positive `tabIndex`.

```js
// eslint.config.js (flat config)
import jsxA11y from "eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y";

export default [
  jsxA11y.flatConfigs.recommended,
  {
    rules: {
      "jsx-a11y/no-autofocus": "warn",
    },
  },
];
```
The type system helps too: a design-system `IconButton` whose props require `label: string` makes the unlabelled icon button impossible to write.

## Layer 2: axe-core everywhere
axe-core is the open-source rules engine from Deque. It runs in the browser against the live DOM, applies rules tagged by WCAG criterion (`wcag2a`, `wcag2aa`, `wcag21aa`, `wcag22aa`, `best-practice`), and reports violations with the failing nodes, an impact level and a help URL. It is designed to avoid false positives: issues it cannot decide are reported as "incomplete" (needs review) rather than failures.

**In component tests (Vitest or Jest with jsdom):**
```tsx
import { render } from "@testing-library/react";
import { axe } from "vitest-axe"; // or jest-axe
import { expect, test } from "vitest";
import { SignupForm } from "./signup-form";

test("signup form has no detectable a11y violations", async () => {
  const { container } = render(<SignupForm />);
  const results = await axe(container);
  expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
});
```
Limitation: jsdom does no layout, so **colour contrast cannot be checked** there (the rule is typically disabled or returns incomplete). Contrast belongs in real-browser tests.

**In end-to-end tests (Playwright):**
```ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";
import AxeBuilder from "@axe-core/playwright";

test("checkout page passes WCAG 2.2 AA automated checks", async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("/checkout");
  await page.getByRole("button", { name: "Continue to payment" }).click();

  const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
    .withTags(["wcag2a", "wcag2aa", "wcag21a", "wcag21aa", "wcag22aa"])
    .exclude("#third-party-chat") // documented, owned elsewhere
    .analyze();

  expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
});
```
Scan pages **in their interesting states**: dialog open, form with errors, menu expanded, dark mode. A scan of the initial load misses most of the app.

**In Storybook**: the accessibility addon runs axe on every story, so component authors see violations while building; Storybook's test runner can fail CI on them.

**Rolling it out on an existing app**: a fresh scan often returns hundreds of violations. Snapshot the current violations as a baseline, fail CI only on new ones, and burn the baseline down by rule (fixing one rule in a shared component often clears hundreds of nodes).

## Lighthouse
Lighthouse's accessibility category runs a subset of axe-core rules and reports a weighted score. It is great for a quick look and for tracking trends, but:
- A **100 score does not mean accessible**. It means the audited subset passed. Lighthouse itself lists "additional items to manually check".
- It audits one page in one state.
- Weighting means some serious failures move the score less than you would expect.
Use axe directly in tests for gating; use Lighthouse (or Lighthouse CI) as a dashboard signal.

## Layer 3: role-based queries in component tests
Testing Library's guiding principle is that tests should resemble how users interact with the software. Its query priority list puts accessible queries first:

```text
1. getByRole           - role + accessible name (the top choice)
2. getByLabelText      - form fields by label
3. getByPlaceholderText, getByText, getByDisplayValue
4. getByAltText, getByTitle
5. getByTestId         - escape hatch
```
`getByRole` queries the accessibility tree that jsdom approximates (roles, names, states). If it cannot find your button by role and name, a screen reader user probably cannot either. That makes every component test a lightweight accessibility test for free.

```tsx
import { render, screen } from "@testing-library/react";
import userEvent from "@testing-library/user-event";

test("disclosure exposes expanded state and toggles with keyboard", async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<Disclosure title="Shipping details">Ships in 2 days</Disclosure>);

  const button = screen.getByRole("button", { name: "Shipping details" });
  expect(button).toHaveAttribute("aria-expanded", "false");

  await user.tab();
  expect(button).toHaveFocus();
  await user.keyboard("{Enter}");

  expect(button).toHaveAttribute("aria-expanded", "true");
  expect(screen.getByText("Ships in 2 days")).toBeVisible();
});

test("invalid email shows an associated error", async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<SignupForm />);
  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText("Email address"), "nope");
  await user.click(screen.getByRole("button", { name: "Create account" }));

  const email = screen.getByRole("textbox", { name: "Email address" });
  expect(email).toHaveAttribute("aria-invalid", "true");
  expect(email).toHaveAccessibleDescription(/enter a valid email/i);
});
```
`toHaveAccessibleName` and `toHaveAccessibleDescription` (from `@testing-library/jest-dom`) assert the computed name and description. Kent C. Dodds' "Common mistakes with React Testing Library" explains why `getByRole` beats test ids and how `screen` and `user-event` keep tests realistic. Playwright uses the same idea with `page.getByRole`, and also supports ARIA snapshot assertions (`toMatchAriaSnapshot`) that compare a YAML representation of the accessibility tree.

## Layer 4: manual keyboard and zoom checks
Five minutes per feature, no tools needed:
1. **Tab through everything.** Every interactive element reachable? Focus always visible? Order logical? Nothing focusable that is hidden?
2. **Operate everything.** Enter and Space on buttons, arrows in composites, Escape closes overlays, focus returns to triggers.
3. **Zoom to 200% and 400%** (or a 320 px wide viewport): content reflows, nothing clipped or overlapping, no horizontal scrolling for text (1.4.10).
4. **Text spacing**: apply the 1.4.12 spacing bookmarklet; nothing breaks.
5. **Reduced motion and forced colours** emulation in DevTools.

## Layer 5: screen reader testing
You do not need to be an expert; you need to be competent with two pairings that cover most users. Screen readers are designed and tested with particular browsers:

| Platform | Pairing | Basics |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Windows | NVDA (free) + Firefox or Chrome | NVDA key is Insert (or Caps Lock). Down arrow reads next item; H / Shift+H headings; D landmarks; F form fields; K links; Tab interactive elements; NVDA+Space toggles browse and focus mode; NVDA+F7 lists elements; Ctrl stops speech |
| macOS | VoiceOver + Safari | Cmd+F5 toggles. VO = Ctrl+Option. VO+Right arrow next item; VO+U opens the rotor (headings, links, landmarks, form controls); VO+Space activates |
| iOS | VoiceOver + Safari | Settings > Accessibility (set the triple-click shortcut). Swipe right/left to move; double-tap to activate; two-finger rotor twist to choose navigation by headings and so on |
| Android | TalkBack + Chrome | Swipe to move, double-tap to activate, reading controls menu |

JAWS remains heavily used, especially in enterprise and government, so for high-stakes products include it or hire testers who use it daily.

**Browse mode versus focus mode** explains many "it works with Tab but not with the screen reader" reports: in browse mode NVDA and JAWS intercept keys for their own navigation (H for headings), and only switch to focus mode, passing keys to the page, when the user enters a form control or an element with an application-type role. Custom widgets with the correct roles get the right mode automatically; `div` soup does not.

A practical screen reader script per feature:
```text
1. Load the page: is the title announced? Navigate by headings (H): does the
   outline make sense? By landmarks (D): main, nav, etc. named?
2. Tab to each control: is role, name and state announced correctly?
   ("Filters, button, collapsed")
3. Operate each control: is the change announced? ("expanded")
4. Trigger dynamic updates: is the status announced exactly once?
5. Submit forms with errors: can you find and fix each error by ear?
6. Open and close dialogs: focus moves in, Escape closes, focus returns.
```
NVDA's speech viewer gives you a text log of what was spoken, useful for bug reports and for demonstrating issues to the team.

## Layer 6: real users
Testing with disabled people who use assistive technology daily finds problems no checklist predicts: confusing labels, tiresome flows, mismatched mental models. Even a few sessions per quarter, recruited through specialised agencies, change product decisions.

## Trade-offs and process
- **Where to gate**: lint and component axe tests block merges; E2E axe on key flows blocks releases; manual screen reader passes for new components and major flows; periodic audits against WCAG 2.2 AA (often by an external firm) to produce a conformance report.
- **Ownership**: a11y defects triaged like other bugs with severity (blocker: cannot complete a task) rather than in a separate backlog that never gets done.
- **Noise**: disabling axe rules to get green builds is the slow failure mode. Every exclusion needs a reason and an owner.

## Common mistakes
- Treating the Lighthouse score as the accessibility status.
- Running axe only on the home page initial state.
- Testing VoiceOver with Chrome (less reliable pairing) and reporting bugs that real users would not hit, or missing ones they would.
- Testing screen readers by tabbing only, never using browse-mode navigation.
- Using `getByTestId` everywhere, which hides missing labels from the test suite.
- Asserting `aria-*` attributes exist instead of asserting the computed name or behaviour.

## In the interview
**What they probe:** do you know automated tools only catch part of the issues, and what does a realistic testing strategy look like? Can you write a role-based test? Have you ever used a screen reader?

**Model 60-second answer ("How do you test accessibility?"):**
1. Layered: jsx-a11y lint, axe in component tests, Playwright plus axe on key flows in their interactive states, Storybook a11y addon.
2. Component tests query by role and name, so missing names fail tests naturally.
3. Automation finds only part (Deque cites about 57% by volume); so a manual keyboard and zoom pass per feature.
4. Screen reader checks with NVDA plus Firefox or Chrome and VoiceOver plus Safari, including mobile; know browse versus focus mode.
5. Periodic audits and user testing with disabled users; baseline existing violations and fail CI only on new ones.

**Follow-ups:** What can axe not detect? Why is Lighthouse 100 not enough? How would you introduce axe into a legacy app with 400 violations? Which screen reader and browser pairs do you test?

deepDives.body:
Most accessibility bugs in apps come from consumers, not from the component internals. An icon button without a label, a dialog without a title, a field whose error is not wired to the input. The kit's job is to make those mistakes **hard to write**.

```tsx
type IconButtonProps = Omit<React.ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement>, "aria-label" | "children"> & {
  label: string;        // required, becomes the accessible name
  icon: React.ComponentType<{ "aria-hidden"?: boolean }>;
};

export function IconButton({ label, icon: Icon, ...rest }: IconButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button type="button" {...rest}>
      <Icon aria-hidden />
      <span className="sr-only">{label}</span>
    </button>
  );
}
```

The same idea applies elsewhere: `Dialog` requires `title`; `Field` owns the ids for label, hint and error so consumers never write `aria-describedby` by hand; `Tabs` takes data (`items: { id, label, panel }[]`) rather than letting consumers assemble roles. Escape hatches exist (`aria-labelledby` override for cases where a visible heading already names a region) but they are explicit and reviewed.

The trade-off is flexibility: a strict API frustrates teams with unusual layouts. The answer is composition slots with required names, not optional names everywhere.
